libcamera: software_isp: apply lens shading to the statistics

The exposure, the white balance and the luminance histogram are computed by
SwStatsCpu from the raw Bayer data, before the debayer shaders apply the lens
shading correction. On a scene with a bright object near the edge of the
frame that made the correction counter-productive: with the gain on, clipping
went from 0.7% to 33% and mean luma from 102 to 134, because the loop never
saw the corners it had just brightened.

SwStatsCpu now takes the same DebayerParams::LensShading values and black
level as the debayer and, when they change, builds the gain of each channel
of a 16x16 block of the input image, with the same formula, strength and
clamp as the shaders. Like them it multiplies the signal above the black
level and leaves the level itself in place, so the pedestal is not stretched,
the white balance still subtracts it from the sums and the histogram keeps
its blacks where they were. A sample then costs three table lookups and three
multiplications before the colour sums, the saturation test of the white
balance and the luminance histogram, and is clipped at the channel maximum so
that a brightened pixel still counts as saturated. The autofocus sharpness
and green sums keep using the raw data. The line functions come in two
variants, chosen per line, so a sensor without a lens shading fit pays
nothing for it.
